PhysicalKeyboard: game keys by their position on a US keyboard, for the Mac driver of the
CrossOver 26.3 engine SWTOR on Mac uses (athei's wine-build cx-26.3.0-8, Wine 11.0,
dlls/winemac.drv). Part of SWTOR on Mac, https://github.com/jdrly/swtor-on-mac.

This patch changes Wine's Mac driver, which is licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public
License 2.1 or later (see the copyright headers of the files it changes); the patch and the
winemac.so built with it are under the same license. Changed by Jan Drlý, 2026-10. Applies to
the sources that engine is built from (CodeWeavers' CrossOver 26.3 with athei's patches):
  https://github.com/athei/wine/tree/d23b39774528237cafe49f9a073286f854b779c0
  (athei/wine@cx-26-patched, named in the release notes of athei/wine-build cx-26.3.0-8)
Built by scripts/build-winemac.sh. The same change as the patch SWTOR on Mac 0.0.4-0.0.6 carried
for CrossOver 24.0.7; only the option declarations moved (CrossOver 26 declares the Mac Driver
options as bool in macdrv_cocoa.h).

New Mac Driver option, OFF by default, per-app configurable like the others:
  HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver                         "PhysicalKeyboard"="y"
  HKCU\Software\Wine\AppDefaults\<app>.exe\Mac Driver   "PhysicalKeyboard"="y"
Off (Wine's behaviour), the driver picks a key's virtual-key code by matching what it types in
the current input source, and names it by what it types: on Czech the number row is VK_1..VK_0
(found through Shift) but GetKeyNameText calls it + ě š č ř ž ý á í é; on French AZERTY & é "
' ( ...; the key labelled Z on Czech or German QWERTZ is VK_Z.
On, every key gets the virtual-key code of its position on a US keyboard (default_map, which
already gives the scan codes), and GetKeyNameText and MapVirtualKey's VK_TO_CHAR (a key's
label) name character keys as on a US keyboard: "1".."0", "Q", "[", ... Text is unchanged:
ToUnicodeEx, VkKeyScanEx and the IME path find the Mac key code through the same table and
translate it with the current layout, so typing still gives the layout's characters (dead
keys, Shift and Option included). Trade-off: everything follows US positions, so on QWERTZ
layouts the key labelled Z is VK_Y and named "Y" (and the other way round).

Test hook (no effect unless set): WINEMAC_TEST_KEYBOARD_LAYOUT=<input source ID> makes a new
thread start with that installed keyboard layout instead of the current one, so tests can
check layouts without changing the system's input source.

